      <h1>How to Register a Trust in Michigan</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Michigan does not require most trusts to be registered. The trustee of a trust administered in Michigan
        <em>may</em> register it with the probate court under MCL § 700.7209 — a short filing that puts the trust's
        existence on the court record. Here is what the process involves and when it's worth doing.
      </p>

      <h2>Is Registration Required?</h2>
      <p>
        For ordinary revocable and irrevocable family trusts, no. Michigan law does not force registration — the
        trust is valid without it. The exceptions are charitable trusts soliciting in Michigan, which must
        register with the Attorney General's Charitable Trust Section (separate from the probate court), and
        certain court-supervised trusts.
      </p>

      <h2>Optional Probate Court Registration — MCL § 700.7209</h2>
      <p>
        Under § 700.7209, the trustee of a trust whose principal place of administration is in Michigan may
        register the trust in the court designated in the trust instrument — or, if none is designated, the probate
        court where the trust is administered. Key facts:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Form:</strong> SCAO Form PC 610 (Registration of Trust), filed with the probate court</li>
        <li><strong>Cost:</strong> $25 filing fee</li>
        <li><strong>Effect:</strong> Registration creates a public record that the trust exists. It does not make
        the trust valid or invalid, does not start court supervision, and the trust document itself is not filed
        with the court.</li>
        <li><strong>Content:</strong> The registration identifies the trust by name and date and lists the
        trustee — not the terms.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>When Registration Helps</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Third parties want confirmation.</strong> A registered trust is easy for a bank or title
        company to verify through the court record.</li>
        <li><strong>Successor trustees take over.</strong> A registration on file simplifies proving authority
        after the original trustee dies or resigns.</li>
        <li><strong>Potential disputes.</strong> A court record fixes a clear, dated record of the trust's
        existence and trustee.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>When You Don't Need It</h2>
      <p>
        If you keep a notarized certificate of trust — which Michigan recognizes in affidavit form under
        MCL § 700.7913 — banks and title companies can rely on it directly, and most families never need
        probate court registration. The certificate lists the trust's name and date, the trustees, their powers,
        and whether the trust is revocable, without revealing the full trust instrument.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/create/certificate-of-trust/">Generate a free Certificate of Trust for Michigan →</a>
      </p>

      <h2>The Steps</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Create and sign your trust instrument (Michigan requires no notary or witnesses for validity —
        MCL §§ 700.7401–700.7402 — though notarization is recommended for real estate).</li>
        <li>Download SCAO Form PC 610 from the Michigan courts website or get it from any probate court.</li>
        <li>File with the probate court in the county where the trust is administered (or the court your trust
        instrument designates) and pay the $25 fee.</li>
        <li>Keep the filed copy with your trust records — you'll show it when institutions ask.</li>
      </ol>

